      text: "By the early twenty-first century, environmental issues had been high on the international agenda for a whole generation of political leaders, government officials, scientists, industrialists, and concerned citizens. Since the late 1960s, awareness of the risks and implications of a wide range of international environmental problems has increased greatly, and justifiably so.\nSince that time, it has become clear that most of the world's seas and oceans are over-fished. Soil is being degraded and eroded on a large scale throughout the world. Natural habitats are being destroyed: for example the area of tropical rainforest has reduced by over 50 per cent since 1950, and the process continues largely unabated. As a result, tens of thousands of species of plants and animals are probably becoming extinct each year. The dumping of waste products into the sea, air, and land means that pollution problems are ubiquitous. Huge quantities of waste, including hazardous chemicals, heavy metals, and radioactive materials, have been dumped at sea, either directly or carried by rivers. Together with sewage and oil spills, these have profoundly damaged sea environments. Billions of people suffer daily air pollution. Acid rain, stratospheric ozone depletion, and climate change are major regional or global problems arising from atmospheric pollution.\nEnvironmental problems are not new. Human societies have long had a major impact on their environment. Widespread industrialisation and rapid population growth changed local problems into truly global environmental crises by the late twentieth century.\nEnvironmental issues first emerged as a focus for international politics in the nineteenth century in the context of resource management agreements. The IMO was formed in 1948 and later given marine pollution responsibilities. Early wildlife treaties were primarily resource-management tools. The 1960s marked a turning point \u2014 Rachel Carson's Silent Spring launched the modern environmental movement; nuclear test bans, oil tanker disasters, and acid rain crises accelerated international environmental law-making. (869 words)",
      marks: 30,
      suggestedAnswer: "Model Pr\u00e9cis (\u2248 290 words):\n\nEnvironmental issues have occupied the international political agenda for over half a century. Since the late 1960s, the scale of ecological damage has become starkly apparent: seas are over-fished, soils eroded, tropical rainforests halved since 1950, and countless species driven to extinction annually. Ubiquitous pollution \u2014 from industrial chemicals and radioactive dumping to oil spills and acid rain \u2014 has devastated marine and terrestrial ecosystems, while billions suffer daily air pollution.\n\nAlthough environmental exploitation is not a modern phenomenon, its consequences were historically localised. Industrialisation and population growth transformed local problems into regional and finally global crises by the twentieth century.\n\nInternational environmental governance developed gradually. Nineteenth-century resource agreements \u2014 originally commercial in purpose \u2014 later absorbed environmental responsibilities. The International Maritime Organization, founded in 1948 as a shipping body, was given anti-pollution duties by 1954. Early wildlife treaties, from the 1889 Berne Convention to the 1946 International Whaling Convention, were primarily resource-management tools, though concern for wildlife's intrinsic value also grew.\n\nThe 1960s marked a turning point. Rachel Carson's Silent Spring galvanised public concern over pesticides and launched the modern environmental movement. Nuclear test bans, oil tanker disasters, acid-rain crises, and unilateral maritime claims all accelerated the pace of international environmental law-making. By the late twentieth century, environmental protection had evolved from isolated bilateral agreements into a comprehensive, multilateral, and genuinely global endeavour.\n\nNote: The model pr\u00e9cis is approximately one-third of the 869-word passage and captures all key points without adding new information."
