    {
      id: "q4_a_2025",
      text: "'The Directive Principles of State Policy have primacy over the Fundamental Rights in the Constitution of India.' Comment.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Constitutional Position:\nThe Constitution has justiciable Fundamental Rights (Part III, Articles 12-35) and non-justiciable Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV, Articles 36-51).\n\nHistorical Evolution:\n- Initial Position (State of Madras v. Champakam Dorairajan, 1951): Fundamental Rights prevail over DPSPs.\n- Kesavananda Bharati (1973): Parliament can amend Fundamental Rights to implement DPSPs but cannot destroy 'basic structure'.\n- 42nd Amendment (1976): Attempted to give DPSPs explicit primacy over Articles 14, 19, 31.\n- Minerva Mills (1980): Supreme Court STRUCK DOWN absolute DPSP primacy as violating basic structure.\n\nCurrent Position (Post-Minerva Mills): Neither FRs nor DPSPs have absolute primacy; courts must HARMONISE them. Basic structure (including FRs) cannot be abrogated even to implement DPSPs.\n\nConclusion: The statement is INCORRECT as a blanket principle. The Constitution envisions harmonious balance \u2014 DPSPs guide policy direction while Fundamental Rights protect individual liberty."
    },
    {
      id: "q4_b_2025",
      text: "'Federalism in India is the key to efficient administration and autonomy to the States.' Critically examine the statement.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "India's Federal Structure: Separate legislative domains (Schedule VII), separate executives, independent revenues \u2014 but with significant centralising tendencies.\n\nArguments Supporting:\n1. Diversity Management: 1.4 billion people, 22 official languages, varied geography requires decentralised governance.\n2. Local Responsiveness: States better positioned to address region-specific needs.\n3. Competitive Federalism: States competing for investment (Gujarat model, Tamil Nadu IT hub) has driven growth.\n4. 73rd/74th Amendments: Transfer of 29 subjects to Panchayati Raj deepened grassroots governance.\n\nChallenges:\n1. Central Dominance: Article 356 (President's Rule), residuary powers with Union, All India Services under central control.\n2. Fiscal Asymmetry: States receive inadequate revenue shares; CSS conditionalities limit spending flexibility.\n3. Governor's Role: Politically appointed Governors used to destabilise state governments.\n4. GST: States argue revenue sharing is inadequate post-2022 compensation period end.\n\nCritical Assessment: India's federalism is 'quasi-federal' \u2014 federal in form, unitary in spirit during emergencies.\n\nConclusion: Federalism is a vital but imperfect mechanism; deepening fiscal and administrative devolution is necessary to realise its full potential."
    },
    {
      id: "q4_c_2025",
      text: "'Financial committees of the Parliament of India ensure financial propriety of the government.' Discuss.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Three Major Financial Committees:\n\n1. Public Accounts Committee (PAC, 1921): India's oldest committee; 22 members (15 LS + 7 RS), chaired by senior opposition member. Examines appropriation accounts, finance accounts, and CAG reports to check whether expenditure was used for sanctioned purposes.\n\n2. Estimates Committee: 30 Lok Sabha members; scrutinises demands for grants before Parliament votes; suggests alternative policies for economy and efficiency.\n\n3. Committee on Public Undertakings: Examines accounts and functioning of PSUs; ensures commercial viability and non-wasteful operation.\n\nRole of CAG: Article 148 makes CAG Parliament's independent auditor; CAG reports form the basis for PAC scrutiny \u2014 the PAC-CAG combination is the cornerstone of parliamentary financial accountability.\n\nEffectiveness: These committees have unearthed significant irregularities (2G spectrum, Commonwealth Games expenditure).\n\nLimitations:\n- Committees can only review expenditure post-hoc, not prevent it.\n- Government compliance with recommendations is not legally mandatory.\n- Parliament rarely devotes adequate time to financial scrutiny.\n\nConclusion: Financial committees are essential accountability instruments but need strengthening through binding compliance mechanisms."
    },
    {
      id: "q4_d_2025",
      text: "Examine the success and failures in the implementation of the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992, effective 24 April 1993):\nAdded Part IX (Articles 243-243O) and 11th Schedule \u2014 gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs).\n\nKey Provisions: Three-tier structure (Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti, Zila Parishad); 5-year elections; 33% women's reservation; State Finance Commissions; State Election Commissions; 29 subjects in 11th Schedule.\n\nSuccesses:\n1. Women's Participation: Over 14 lakh elected women representatives \u2014 many states exceed 33% reservation.\n2. Grassroots Governance: Real decentralisation in Kerala (People's Plan Campaign) and Karnataka.\n3. Social Inclusion: SC/ST reservations brought historically marginalised communities into governance.\n4. MGNREGA Implementation: PRIs serve as implementing agencies.\n\nFailures:\n1. Incomplete Devolution: Most states have not transferred adequate functions, finances, and functionaries (3Fs).\n2. Financial Dependence: PRIs lack independent revenues; heavily dependent on grants.\n3. Proxy Representation: Sarpanch-pati phenomenon \u2014 husbands exercise power instead of elected women.\n4. Bureaucratic Dominance: Block Development Officers retain de facto control.\n\nConclusion: A constitutional revolution whose transformative potential is only partially realised \u2014 requiring consistent political will from states."
    },
    {
      id: "q4_e_2025",
      text: "Assess the role of BRICS to build an alternative credible financial institution to serve the present world economic order.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "BRICS (now BRICS+ with UAE, Ethiopia, Egypt, Iran): ~36% of global GDP (PPP), 40%+ of world population.\n\nAlternative Financial Institutions:\n\n1. New Development Bank (NDB, 2014): Headquartered in Shanghai; $100 billion capitalisation; equal voting rights among original members; approved $33+ billion in infrastructure loans (energy, transport, water) by 2024. Funds developing nations without IMF/World Bank conditionalities.\n\n2. Contingent Reserve Arrangement (CRA, 2014): $100 billion liquidity support \u2014 BRICS alternative to IMF balance of payments assistance.\n\n3. De-dollarisation Efforts: Increased bilateral trade in local currencies; BRICS reserve currency under discussion (limited progress).\n\nSuccesses:\n- NDB demonstrates developing-world infrastructure financing capacity\n- Expands options beyond Bretton Woods institutions\n\nLimitations:\n- NDB still relies heavily on dollar-denominated loans\n- India-China border tensions limit institutional coherence\n- No BRICS common currency has materialised\n- CRA has never been fully activated\n\nConclusion: BRICS institutions supplement rather than replace the Bretton Woods order, representing an important step toward a more multipolar global financial architecture."
    }
