      id: "q1_a_2024",
      text: "Why did the Harappan civilization degenerate and finally collapse?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Decline of Harappan Civilisation (c. 1900 BCE):\n\n1. Climate Change (Primary): Weakening of Indian Summer Monsoon \u2014 drying of the Ghaggar-Hakra (Saraswati) river system, on whose banks many sites were located (Giosan et al., 2012).\n\n2. River Course Changes: Tectonic activity shifted Indus tributaries, disrupting agricultural and trade networks.\n\n3. Trade Network Collapse: Decline of maritime trade with Mesopotamia after 2000 BCE reduced economic prosperity.\n\n4. Epidemic Disease: Mass burial sites at Mohenjo-daro suggest possible epidemic in later phases.\n\n5. Aryan Invasion (Now Largely Discredited): Wheeler's theory of violent invasion is contradicted by genetic and archaeological evidence showing complex gradual population movement.\n\n6. Internal Factors: Absence of strong centralised authority may have made coordinated responses to ecological stress difficult.\n\nConclusion: The decline was likely driven by climate change interacting with ecological, economic, and social vulnerabilities \u2014 a process of devolution rather than sudden catastrophic collapse."
