    {
      id: "q1_d_2024",
      text: "Do you agree that the formation of the Indian National Congress was in the air even if A.O. Hume had not taken any initiative?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Yes \u2014 INC's Formation Was Historically Inevitable:\n\nPre-existing Organisations: British Indian Association (1851), Madras Mahajan Sabha (1884), Bombay Presidency Association (1885) \u2014 proto-nationalist bodies; an all-India umbrella was the natural next step.\n\nEducated Indian Class: Western education created a politically conscious English-educated class (Gokhale, Pherozeshah Mehta, Surendranath Banerjea) who would have found an alternative institutional channel.\n\nColonial Contradictions: Ilbert Bill controversy (1883), Arms Act, racial discrimination in civil services, and economic drain policies had already created political ferment.\n\nCommunication Revolution: Railways and telegraph enabled pan-Indian political coordination for the first time.\n\nHume's Contribution: He provided organisational impetus, vice-regal approval (Dufferin's blessing), and a non-threatening face \u2014 ensuring the colonial government did not immediately suppress the new organisation. He accelerated and shaped its institutional form.\n\nConclusion: The formation of a national political body was virtually inevitable. Hume's initiative accelerated and shaped INC's specific form; Surendranath Banerjea's Indian National Association (1876) could have evolved into an equivalent national congress."
    },
    {
      id: "q1_e_2024",
      text: "What is the significance of the Rowlatt Satyagraha of 1919?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Significance of Rowlatt Satyagraha (6 April 1919):\n\nBackground: Rowlatt Act (1919) \u2014 based on Sedition Committee Report \u2014 allowed indefinite imprisonment without trial. Gandhi called it the 'Black Act'.\n\n1. First All-India Mass Movement: First time nationalist agitation was coordinated simultaneously across India \u2014 cities, towns, and rural areas.\n\n2. Gandhi's Political Debut: Established Gandhi as undisputed leader of Indian nationalism; demonstrated that satyagraha (non-violent mass civil disobedience) could be a powerful weapon.\n\n3. Amritsar Massacre: Jallianwala Bagh (13 April 1919) \u2014 General Dyer ordered troops to fire on peaceful gathering, killing 379+ (official) to 1,000+ (unofficial). Shook Indian faith in British justice fundamentally.\n\n4. National Solidarity: First time Hindus and Muslims, upper and lower castes worked together in a common cause \u2014 foreshadowing the Non-Cooperation Movement coalition.\n\n5. Shift in Political Tone: Transformed constitutional moderates into radical nationalists. Rabindranath Tagore returned his knighthood in protest.\n\nConclusion: A watershed that transformed Indian nationalism from elite constitutional agitation to mass non-violent direct action."
    }
