      suggestedAnswer: "Uneven Industrial Distribution in India:\n\nHistorical: British-established ports (Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) became industrial centres. Cotton mills in Mumbai, jute mills in Kolkata created lasting industrial geography.\n\nRaw Material Proximity: Iron and steel in Jharkhand/Odisha/Chhattisgarh (Tata Steel-Jamshedpur, SAIL-Rourkela/Bhilai); sugar in UP/Bihar near sugarcane areas.\n\nInfrastructure: Maharashtra and Gujarat have better port, road, rail, and power infrastructure attracting diverse industries.\n\nSkilled Labour: IT/software concentrated in Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai where engineering graduates are abundant.\n\nCapital: Mumbai as financial capital reduces financing costs for nearby industries.\n\nGovernment Policy: SEZs and industrial corridors (Delhi-Mumbai Industrial Corridor, Chennai-Bengaluru Industrial Corridor) create deliberate clusters.\n\nExamples of Concentration:\n- Automobiles: Pune, Chennai, Gurgaon-Manesar\n- Pharmaceuticals: Hyderabad ('Pharma City'), Ahmedabad\n- Diamonds: Surat processes 90% of world's diamonds\n- Textiles: Tirupur, Ludhiana, Ichalkaranji\n\nConsequence: GSDP per capita in Maharashtra (~\u20b92.8 lakh) vs. Bihar (~\u20b945,000) \u2014 severe regional inequality."
