      id: "q2_d_2024",
      text: "'Agriculture is an environment-dependent activity.' Discuss.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Two-Way Relationship: Agriculture depends fundamentally on environment, while also being a major driver of environmental change.\n\nHow Environment Shapes Agriculture:\n1. Climate/Rainfall: 54% of net sown area is unirrigated \u2014 'gamble in the monsoon'. El-Ni\u00f1o droughts (2002, 2009, 2023) devastate yields. Wheat yield declines 6% per 1\u00b0C rise.\n2. Soil: Black soil (Maharashtra-MP) for cotton; alluvial soil (Indo-Gangetic plain) for wheat-rice; red laterite for cashew, rubber.\n3. Topography: Terraced farming in hills; flat plains for mechanised agriculture.\n4. Water: Groundwater depletion (Punjab, Haryana) threatens long-term sustainability.\n\nAgriculture's Environmental Impact:\n- 16% of India's GHG emissions from agriculture (methane from paddy, N\u2082O from fertilisers)\n- Groundwater over-extraction causing land subsidence\n- Stubble burning causing severe Delhi air pollution\n- Chemical fertiliser runoff causing water body eutrophication\n\nClimate Change Impact: Crop calendar shifts, pest range expansion, increased extreme weather events.\n\nPolicy: PMKSY (water efficiency), Soil Health Cards, Zero Budget Natural Farming address the nexus.\n\nConclusion: Sustainable agriculture requires treating environmental conservation and food security as complementary objectives."
