      id: "q2_2023",
      text: "The Earth is a home for a rich and diverse array of living organisms, whose genetic diversity and relationship with each other, constitute the planet's biodiversity. It includes species as well as varieties found within the same species, as also the enormous ecosystems and habitats: forests, grasslands, oceans and its depths, peats and peat bogs. Worldwide, an area of tropical forest equal to a football field, is disappearing every second. Forests are being cleared for agriculture, cattle ranching and for timber. They get submerged when dams are constructed. When forests are felled we deal a blow to biodiversity, the richness of life. Five great waves of extinction have decimated our planet's species in the remote past. The sixth wave, which we are witnessing, is largely man made. One-fourth of the Earth's total biological diversity, amounting to about 1.6 million species, which might be useful to mankind in one way or the other, is in the serious risk of existence and if we ignore the biodiversity crisis, we would lose these species in the next 25 years. If we respond with knowledge and technology, we might hold the loss to 10 percent, but even this amounts to millions of species.\nIt is a well-known fact the ecosystem is undergoing changes due to pollution, invasive species, over-exploitation by humans, climate change and many other reasons. The importance of species diversity conservation is justified by the direct use of species as food sources, fibre, fuel, new chemicals and raw materials. When a species is lost, it is lost for ever. Our ethics tell us that we should not deprive future generations from the economic and aesthetic benefits derived from biodiversity. Top priority should be given to check destruction of habitats, as it is most responsible for biodiversity loss.\nConservation of biodiversity involves protection of genes and species in their populations, ecosystems or habitats. The diversity of genes within a species increases its adaptability to pollution, diseases, stresses and environmental changes. When varieties are destroyed the genetic diversity within the species is diminished.\nThere are two approaches: in situ (on site) conservation \u2014 protecting species in their natural habitats, and ex situ (off site) conservation \u2014 protecting species away from their natural habitat in zoos, gene banks, and botanical gardens. (673 words)",
      marks: 30,
      suggestedAnswer: "Model Pr\u00e9cis (\u2248 225 words):\n\nThe Earth's biodiversity \u2014 encompassing genes, species, and ecosystems \u2014 faces unprecedented crisis. Tropical forests disappear at the rate of one football field per second due to agriculture, logging, and dam construction. A human-caused sixth mass extinction is underway, threatening one-fourth of all known species within 25 years if unchecked. Even optimal technological intervention may not prevent the loss of millions of species.\n\nMultiple threats \u2014 pollution, invasive species, over-exploitation, and climate change \u2014 are degrading ecosystems worldwide. Each species lost is irreplaceable; our ethical obligation to future generations demands that we conserve biodiversity's economic and aesthetic benefits. Habitat destruction is the primary driver and therefore the primary target for conservation action.\n\nConserving genetic diversity within species is equally critical, as it enhances adaptability to environmental stress, disease, and pollution. Reducing genetic diversity weakens a species' capacity to survive future challenges.\n\nTwo complementary conservation strategies exist: in situ conservation, which protects species in their natural habitats, and ex situ conservation, which preserves them outside their natural range in zoos, gene banks, and botanical gardens. Combining both approaches, backed by individual responsibility, organisational commitment, and strong governance, is essential to safeguard Earth's biological heritage.\n\nNote: Approximately one-third of the 673-word original, written in the candidate's own words."
