      id: "q4_c_2023",
      text: "The Indian Constitution is enriched by the Constitutions of other countries. Elaborate.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Indian Constitution's Borrowings (Key Examples):\n\n| Feature | Source |\n|---|---|\n| Parliamentary system, Cabinet government, Rule of Law | United Kingdom |\n| Fundamental Rights, Judicial Review, Independent Judiciary | United States |\n| Directive Principles of State Policy | Ireland |\n| Emergency provisions | Germany (Weimar) |\n| Concurrent List, Centre-State relations | Australia |\n| Amendment procedure (partly rigid, partly flexible) | South Africa |\n| Five-Year Plans | USSR |\n| Fundamental Duties | Former USSR/Soviet |\n\nKey Adaptations \u2014 Not Mere Copying:\n- Parliamentary system from UK but WITH a written constitution (unlike UK)\n- Judicial Review from US but with stronger parliamentary component\n- DPSP from Ireland but made non-justiciable due to India's economic constraints in 1950\n- Emergency from Weimar but with parliamentary checks added after 1975 Emergency\n\nAmbedkar's View: 'There is nothing to be ashamed of in borrowing' \u2014 the achievement lies in adapting universal democratic principles to India's particular federal, plural, and diverse context.\n\nOriginal Indian Contributions: Anti-defection provisions; Schedules for SC/ST reservations; Fundamental Duties (42nd Amendment); detailed election commission provisions.\n\nConclusion: The Indian Constitution's genius lies in synthesising global democratic wisdom into a framework suited to one of the world's most complex societies."
