      id: "q4_e_2023",
      text: "'India's foreign policy has moved from non-alignment to multi-alignment.' Discuss.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Non-Alignment (1947\u20131991): Nehru-led policy to navigate Cold War bipolarity by staying unaffiliated with US or Soviet blocs. NAM co-founded 1961 (Belgrade). In practice, India leaned toward USSR (1971 Treaty of Peace, Friendship and Cooperation).\n\nLimitations: Post-Cold War, NAM's relevance declined. Economic liberalisation (1991) required engagement with all major powers.\n\nMulti-Alignment (Post-2000, Accelerated Post-2014):\n1. USA: COMCASA, BECA, LEMOA defence agreements; Quad membership (India-US-Japan-Australia); I2U2 grouping; defence industrial cooperation.\n2. Russia: S-400 purchase; continued diplomatic engagement; oil imports at concessional rates post-Ukraine conflict.\n3. China: BRICS and SCO membership; bilateral trade (China is India's largest trading partner) alongside military standoff at LAC.\n4. Gulf States: UAE CEPA; Saudi Arabia for energy and diaspora interests.\n5. EU, Japan, Australia: Strategic and economic partnerships; Indo-Pacific cooperation.\n\nKey Concept \u2014 Strategic Autonomy: Ability to act in national interest without being a client of any major power. Explains India's abstention on Russia-Ukraine UN resolutions.\n\nCriticism: Can create contradictions (engaging US and Russia simultaneously); limited influence when India takes no clear positions on major conflicts.\n\nConclusion: Multi-alignment better serves India's interests in a multipolar world, provided India has the diplomatic capacity to manage complex simultaneous relationships."
