      id: "q1_d_2022",
      text: "Evaluate the role of Indian National Army (INA) in the freedom struggle of India.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Indian National Army (INA) \u2014 Role in Freedom Struggle:\n\nFormation and Leadership:\n- First INA formed in Malaya (1942) by Mohan Singh after fall of Singapore.\n- Reconstituted by Subhas Chandra Bose (Netaji) in Singapore (1943) \u2014 led by 'Azad Hind Fauj'; slogan 'Jai Hind'; motto 'Unity, Faith, Sacrifice'.\n- Women's regiment: Rani of Jhansi Regiment led by Lakshmi Sahgal.\n\nMilitary Contribution:\n- Fought alongside Japanese forces; advanced to Imphal and Kohima (Manipur) in 1944 \u2014 farthest advance toward India.\n- Initial successes in Arakan; eventually defeated due to Japanese defeat and supply failures.\n\nPolitical and Psychological Impact (More Significant Than Military):\n1. INA Trials (1945-46): British tried INA officers (Dhillon, Sahgal, Shah Nawaz) for treason in Red Fort \u2014 backfired spectacularly. Nehru appeared in court in barrister robes \u2014 united all parties in INA's defence.\n2. Naval Mutiny (1946): RIN (Royal Indian Navy) mutiny partly inspired by INA spirit \u2014 Bombay, Karachi sailors rebelled. Demonstrated collapse of British authority over Indian armed forces.\n3. Morale and Symbol: INA demonstrated that Hindu and Muslim soldiers could fight together under Indian command \u2014 undermined Two-Nation theory and British argument about Indians' inability to govern themselves.\n4. Accelerated Transfer of Power: Atlee later acknowledged INA's impact on the decision to transfer power \u2014 Britain could no longer rely on Indian forces to maintain colonial rule.\n\nConclusion: INA's direct military role was limited, but its psychological, political, and symbolic impact was decisive in accelerating India's independence."
