      id: "q1_c_2022",
      text: "Discuss the contribution of Sufis to Indian society and culture.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Sufi Contribution to Indian Society and Culture:\n\nMajor Orders in India:\n- Chishti (most prominent): Moinuddin Chishti (Ajmer), Qutbuddin Bakhtiyar Kaki (Delhi), Nizamuddin Auliya (Delhi), Amir Khusrau (Delhi) \u2014 emphasis on love, devotion, music (sama/qawwali).\n- Suhrawardi: Ahmad Sirhindi (later, Naqshbandi) \u2014 orthodox critique of Akbar's Din-i-Ilahi.\n- Qadiri, Naqshbandi: More orthodox; later orders.\n\nSocial Contributions:\n1. Equality: Khangahs (hospices) open to all \u2014 regardless of caste or religion. Langar (free kitchen) practised by Chishti saints \u2014 preceded Sikh langar tradition.\n2. Social Reform: Opposed untouchability; welcomed low-caste devotees; challenged social hierarchy through mystical experience as the great equaliser.\n3. Syncretic Hindu-Muslim Culture: Drew on Vedanta, Bhakti devotionalism, and Islamic mysticism \u2014 created shared spiritual vocabulary.\n\nCultural Contributions:\n1. Music: Qawwali \u2014 fusion of Persian music with Indian instruments; Hindi/Braj Bhasha lyrics. Amir Khusrau invented or popularised: tabla, sitar (debated), khayal singing style.\n2. Literature: Amir Khusrau's poetry in Persian and Hindi \u2014 created Khariboli Hindi (later Hindustani); Sufi literature popularised vernacular languages over Sanskrit/Arabic.\n3. Architecture: Dargahs (shrines) \u2014 Ajmer Dargah, Nizamuddin Dargah \u2014 became centres of communal harmony and pilgrimage across faiths.\n\nConclusion: Sufism was one of the most powerful forces for social integration and cultural synthesis in medieval India \u2014 its legacy of pluralism continues in Indian composite culture."
