      id: "q1_e_2022",
      text: "'Non-cooperation Movement and the Civil Disobedience Movement mark a strategic shift in the freedom struggle.' Discuss.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Strategic Shift \u2014 From Moderate to Mass Nationalism:\n\nPre-1920 Congress: 'Moderates' dominated \u2014 memorials, petitions, constitutional agitation. Relied on British goodwill and educated elite. Gopal Krishna Gokhale's approach: 'Graduated self-governance.'\n\nNon-Cooperation Movement (1920-22):\n- First truly mass movement; Gandhiji mobilised peasants, workers, students, and merchants.\n- Strategy: Non-violent non-cooperation \u2014 boycott of councils, courts, educational institutions, and foreign cloth; Khilafat alliance (Hindu-Muslim unity).\n- New class: Urban middle class, lawyers, teachers participated alongside farmers.\n- Halted after Chauri Chaura violence (Feb 1922) \u2014 showed Gandhi's commitment to non-violence over political expediency.\n\nCivil Disobedience Movement (1930-34):\n- Salt March (1930) \u2014 confronted the most symbolically oppressive colonial law; proved mass civil disobedience viable without violence.\n- First substantial female participation; rural India mobilised.\n- Dharasana Salt Works raid \u2014 passive resistance before brutal lathi charges; international press coverage transformed world opinion.\n- Gandhi-Irwin Pact (1931) \u2014 government negotiated with Gandhi as equal.\n\nStrategic Shifts Marked:\n1. Method: From petition to action \u2014 satyagraha replaced prayer.\n2. Constituency: From elite to mass \u2014 farmers, women, untouchables included.\n3. Goal: From reform within Empire to Purna Swaraj (complete independence, declared 1929).\n4. Leadership: Gandhiji unified nationalist, peasant, and religious streams.\n\nConclusion: These movements transformed the Indian National Congress from a middle-class pressure group into a genuinely mass national organisation \u2014 a fundamental strategic and structural shift."
