      id: "q2_a_2022",
      text: "Why are the farmers of India still haunted by recurrence of drought and flood despite river valley planning?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Paradox of Flood and Drought Coexisting:\n\nRiver Valley Projects (Dams and Irrigation):\n- India has 5,200+ major and medium dams \u2014 world's third largest.\n- Bhakra-Nangal, Hirakud, Damodar Valley, etc. designed for flood control, irrigation, and power.\n\nWhy Droughts Persist Despite River Projects:\n1. Uneven Distribution: Irrigation projects benefit certain regions disproportionately \u2014 Punjab, Haryana, UP well-irrigated; Vidarbha, Bundelkhand, Marathwada remain rain-dependent.\n2. Groundwater Depletion: Green Revolution's tube-well irrigation depleted aquifers \u2014 Punjab/Haryana water tables falling 1-2 m/year. Surface irrigation cannot compensate.\n3. Monsoon Dependence: 60% of Indian agriculture is still rain-fed; monsoon variability (La Ni\u00f1a/El Ni\u00f1o) causes droughts in unirrigated areas.\n4. Siltation of Reservoirs: Dams lose 30-50% of storage capacity to siltation \u2014 reducing flood control and irrigation capacity.\n\nWhy Floods Persist:\n1. Deforestation: Himalayan deforestation increases runoff; rivers carry more silt and flood plains faster.\n2. Encroachment: Flood plains and wetlands (natural buffers) encroached upon.\n3. Inadequate River Maintenance: Rivers straightened, narrowed \u2014 reduced absorption capacity.\n4. Climate Change: Intense, concentrated rainfall events increasing \u2014 dam-based systems not designed for such extremes.\n\nConclusion: River valley planning addressed the problem partially but is undermined by siltation, uneven coverage, groundwater depletion, encroachment, and climate change."
