      id: "q2_d_2022",
      text: "Assess the prospect of food processing industry with suitable examples from India.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Food Processing Industry \u2014 India's Prospects:\n\nCurrent Status: India's food processing industry valued at ~$300 billion (2023), 6th largest in the world. Contribution to GDP: 8.8%; to manufacturing: 13%; employs 18+ million directly.\n\nGrowth Drivers:\n1. Agricultural Base: India is the world's largest producer of milk, pulses, spices, bananas, mangoes, and the 2nd largest in fruits and vegetables.\n2. Domestic Market: 1.4 billion consumers; rising middle class (300+ million) demanding processed, packaged, and ready-to-eat foods.\n3. Export Potential: Agricultural exports of $53 billion (2022-23); processed food export growing at 12% per annum.\n4. Government Support: PLI (Production-Linked Incentive) for food processing (\u20b910,900 crore); Kisan SAMPADA Yojana \u2014 cold chain, logistics, processing clusters.\n5. FDI: 100% FDI allowed in food processing \u2014 Nestl\u00e9, Unilever, Pepsi, Britannia, Marico well-established.\n\nChallenges:\n1. Post-harvest Losses: 15-35% of fruits and vegetables lost due to poor cold chain.\n2. Fragmented Supply Chain: Smallholder agriculture (average 1.1 ha holding) creates aggregation problems.\n3. Regulatory Complexity: FSSAI standards, multiple licences required.\n\nSuccess Examples:\n- Amul: Dairy cooperative model \u2014 \u20b972,000 crore revenue (2022-23)\n- ITC: Agri-business and food processing integration\n- Mother Dairy, Dabur, Patanjali: Indigenous brands scaling nationally\n\nConclusion: Food processing is a sunrise sector with transformative potential for farmer income, employment, and export earnings \u2014 provided supply chain and regulatory challenges are resolved."
