      id: "q4_d_2020",
      text: "What are the constitutional provisions for the amendment of the Indian Constitution?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Constitutional Amendment \u2014 Article 368:\n\nThree Types of Amendment Procedures:\n\n1. Simple Majority (Not Article 368 \u2014 ordinary legislation):\n- Admission of new states (Article 2); formation of new states (Article 3); abolishing or creating Legislative Councils; second schedule changes.\n- Simple majority in Parliament (> 50% of members present and voting).\n\n2. Special Majority (Article 368 \u2014 Most amendments):\n- Two-thirds majority of members present and voting AND\n- Majority of total membership of each House.\n- No state ratification required.\n- Examples: Fundamental Rights, Directive Principles, constitutional amendments generally.\n\n3. Special Majority + Ratification by Half the States:\n- Two-thirds majority in each House + ratification by at least half of state legislatures.\n- Applies to: Federal structure \u2014 election of President (Article 54, 55); Supreme Court (Article 124); HC jurisdiction (Article 130, 214, 217); distribution of legislative powers (Articles 245-255); representation of states in Parliament; Article 368 itself.\n\nProcedure:\n1. Bill introduced in either House (not joint session; no money bill).\n2. Passed by requisite majority in each House separately.\n3. Presidential assent (President cannot withhold assent \u2014 Article 368(2) proviso after 24th Amendment).\n4. State ratification if required.\n\nLimitations (Kesavananda Bharati, 1973): Basic structure cannot be amended \u2014 Parliament's amending power under Article 368 is not absolute.\n\nNumber of Amendments: 106 as of 2024 \u2014 reflecting active constitutional evolution."
