    {
      id: "q2_2018",
      text: "Education has not been of the kind that can help you towards happiness. It has not been of the kind that can make you capable of freedom. The aim of education in a democracy must be the same for all the people. Education must aim at making them all capable of freedom, for they are all to be free. Whatever education will achieve, this aim must be given to every person in proportion to his capacity to receive it. If education is necessary for one, it must be necessary for all. Only in this way can we hope to prevent the exploitation of one group by another. Only in this way can we develop a true community. The citizens must be able to understand one another. They must have a common culture. They must have the intellectual training needed to comprehend it and to communicate with those who share it.\nEducation for freedom consists in transmitting to the rising generation the civilisation they have inherited, together with the techniques by which it may be understood. No man or woman is equipped to be a ruler without this education.\nAs liberal education helps us to establish a free community, so does it to preserve it. As Thomas F. Woodlock has said: \"Democracy rests ultimately upon 'public opinion' as its base. Public opinion follows upon free speech, free interchange of ideas, of judgements, of opinions; it is generated by these things. Men interchange these things by words. Sound logic it was that put grammar, logic, and rhetoric as preliminary to geometry, arithmetic, music, and astronomy in the scheme of the seven liberal arts in an age which was notable for the clearness of its thinking and the exactitude of its expression. Both these things are absolutely necessary for the functioning of democracy as the best form of government. If we are going to 'educate for democracy', we had better find the right way to teach them.\" And the ability to read and write is the best defence against anti-democratic propaganda.\nIn addition to all the above merits, liberal education has yet another: it is the best vocational training. The ideal education for freedom is a liberal education in the liberal arts and the cultural heritage of the country.\nIf we cannot give liberal education to every citizen in proportion to his capacity to receive it we might as well give up our hopes of achieving a democratic community. Individual happiness, good citizenship, and the improvement of society all depend on our success in helping the people to achieve liberal education. What the world needs, what the country must have, is free minds. It means minds committed to the general good by good moral and intellectual habits. (770 words approx.)",
      marks: 30,
      suggestedAnswer: "Model Pr\u00e9cis (\u2248 257 words):\n\nExisting education has failed in two respects: it does not lead to happiness and it does not equip citizens for freedom. In a democracy, where freedom is universal, education towards freedom must also be universal \u2014 given to every person in proportion to their capacity. Only such universal education can prevent exploitation and build genuine community, for citizens must share a common culture and the intellectual capacity to communicate across it.\n\nFreedom-oriented education transmits civilisation alongside the analytical tools for understanding it. Liberal education \u2014 which Woodlock shows grounds democracy in articulate public opinion \u2014 is the foundation of the democratic process. Democracy requires citizens who can think clearly and express themselves precisely; grammar, logic, and rhetoric serve democracy as directly as science does. Literacy is simultaneously the citizen's defence against propaganda and the prerequisite for free political discourse.\n\nLiberal education is also, paradoxically, the best vocational preparation \u2014 training minds rather than merely hands. Without it, a democratic community is impossible to sustain; with it, individual happiness, good citizenship, and social improvement become achievable.\n\nThe conclusion is uncompromising: if liberal education cannot be given to all citizens proportionate to their capacity, democratic community is a pretence. Society needs not minds loaded with obsolete information but minds governed by good moral and intellectual habits \u2014 minds informed by the accumulated wisdom of human experience and capable of functioning well under any circumstances. Education is not ornamental; it is the essential condition of freedom. (240 words)"
    }
