    {
      id: "q2_2017",
      text: "Two principal forms of the constitution are known to history \u2014 one is called unitary and the other is federal. The two essential characteristics of a unitary constitution are: (1) the supremacy of the central polity, and (2) the absence of subsidiary sovereign polities. On the contrary, a federal constitution is marked: (1) by the existence of a central polity and subsidiary polities side by side, and (2) by each being sovereign in the field assigned to it. In other words, federation means the establishment of a dual polity.\nThe draft constitution is a federal constitution inasmuch as it establishes what may be a dual polity. This dual polity under the proposed constitution will consist of the Union at the centre and the States at the periphery, each endowed with sovereign powers to be exercised in the field assigned to them respectively by the constitution. This dual polity resembles the American Constitution. The American polity is also a dual polity \u2014 the Federal government and the governments of the States. Under the American Constitution, the Federal government is not a mere league of the States nor are the States administrative units or agencies of the Federal government. In the same way, the Indian Constitution proposed in the draft constitution is not a league of States nor are the States administrative units of the Union government. Here, however, the similarities between the Indian and American Constitutions come to an end.\nThe points of difference between the American federation and the Indian federation are mainly two. In the USA, dual polity is followed by dual citizenship \u2014 citizenship of the USA and citizenship of the State. No doubt the rigours of this double citizenship are much assuaged by the Fourteenth Amendment, which prohibits the States from taking away the rights of United States citizens. At the same time, in certain political matters, including the right to vote and to hold public office, the States may and do discriminate in favour of their own citizens. In short, there are a number of rights that a State can grant to its own citizens that it legally denies to non-residents.\nThe proposed Indian Constitution is a dual polity with a single citizenship. There is only one citizenship for the whole of India. It is Indian citizenship. There is no State citizenship. Every Indian has the same rights of citizenship, no matter in what State he resides.\nThe dual polity of the proposed Indian Constitution also differs from the USA in another respect. In the USA, the Constitutions of the Federal and the State governments are loosely connected. In India, the connections are tight \u2014 the Union Constitution and State constitutions are parts of one integrated constitutional document. (781 words)",
      marks: 30,
      suggestedAnswer: "Model Pr\u00e9cis (\u2248 260 words):\n\nConstitutional systems are broadly of two types: unitary \u2014 in which a single central polity is supreme and no subsidiary sovereign unit exists \u2014 and federal \u2014 in which central and regional polities coexist, each sovereign within its constitutionally assigned field.\n\nThe proposed Indian Constitution is federal: it creates a dual polity of a Union at the centre and States at the periphery, each exercising sovereignty in its own domain. This resembles the American Constitution, under which neither the Federal government nor the State governments are subordinate to each other \u2014 each is independently authoritative within its sphere.\n\nHowever, significant differences distinguish Indian and American federalism. In the United States, dual polity is accompanied by dual citizenship \u2014 national and state \u2014 giving individual states considerable power to confer special advantages on their own citizens in matters such as employment, professional licensing, hunting and fishing licences, university tuition, and hospital admission. An outsider is everywhere under some legal disadvantage relative to a state's own citizens.\n\nThe proposed Indian Constitution, by contrast, combines dual polity with single citizenship. All Indians share a common citizenship regardless of the state in which they reside, and no state may grant its citizens rights that are denied to citizens from other states.\n\nA further distinction is structural: the American federal and state constitutions are separate, loosely connected documents, whereas the Indian Constitution integrates both in a single constitutional text \u2014 creating a tighter, more unified federal system."
    }
