    {
      id: "q3_b_2017",
      text: "What is Green Energy? Explain the policies envisaged by the Government of India to promote Green Energy sources.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Green Energy: Energy produced from sources that generate minimal greenhouse gas emissions and are renewable \u2014 distinguishing from fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas) which emit CO\u2082.\n\nMain Green Energy Sources:\n1. Solar: Photovoltaic and concentrated solar.\n2. Wind: Onshore and offshore.\n3. Hydroelectric: Small hydro (large hydro excluded from many green definitions due to ecological impact).\n4. Biomass and Biogas: Organic waste converted to energy.\n5. Green Hydrogen: Hydrogen produced by electrolysis using renewable electricity.\n\nGovernment Policies (2017 Context and Since):\n1. National Solar Mission (JNNSM, 2010): Target 100 GW solar by 2022 (extended to 2023); achieved 72 GW by 2023.\n2. National Wind Energy Mission: 140 GW by 2030; offshore wind policy.\n3. International Solar Alliance (ISA): India-France initiative (2015) \u2014 120 countries; promote solar in tropical regions.\n4. Renewable Purchase Obligation (RPO): Discoms required to purchase fixed % of power from renewables.\n5. KUSUM (Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthan Mahabhiyan): Solar pumps for farmers; solarisation of grid-connected pumps.\n6. National Green Hydrogen Mission (2023): Target 5 MMT green hydrogen by 2030; $101 billion investment.\n7. PM KMIN (Micro Irrigation Fund): Efficient irrigation reduces agricultural energy demand.\n8. Production-Linked Incentive (PLI) for Solar: Domestic manufacturing of solar modules \u2014 reduce China dependence.\n9. DISCM Financial Reform: Green energy transition requires solvent DISCOMs \u2014 RDSS scheme.\n\nConclusion: India has moved from fossil fuel dependence to becoming a global renewable leader \u2014 the 500 GW renewable target by 2030 reflects strategic transformation of the energy sector."
    },
    {
      id: "q3_c_2017",
      text: "In India, do you agree growth of service sector is sustainable and will it ensure equity in terms of creating employment?",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "India's Service Sector Growth \u2014 Sustainability and Equity Assessment:\n\nService Sector: 55%+ of GDP; includes IT, banking, insurance, trade, transport, government, real estate.\n\nIs Growth Sustainable?\nArguments For:\n1. Human Capital: India's English-speaking, technical graduate population \u2014 globally competitive in IT, finance, consulting.\n2. Global Demand: Digital economy \u2014 cloud, AI, IT services \u2014 demand growing globally; Indian IT well-positioned.\n3. Domestic Market: 1.4 billion consumers driving retail, banking, healthcare, telecom services.\n4. Formalisation: Digitisation of services (UPI, e-commerce) formalising previously unrecorded activity.\n\nArguments Against:\n1. AI Threat: Automation threatening repetitive IT tasks \u2014 BPO, back-office work at risk.\n2. Infrastructure Dependence: Reliable internet, power, and logistics required; gaps remain in Tier-2/3 cities.\n3. Global Competition: Vietnam, Philippines, Eastern Europe competing in IT services.\n\nDoes It Ensure Equity in Employment?\nMajor Problem \u2014 NO:\n1. Skill Mismatch: High-growth services (IT, finance, consulting) require tertiary education \u2014 only 30% of India's population has it. Services cannot absorb India's vast semi-skilled and unskilled labour pool.\n2. Premature Deindustrialisation Risk: India moving from agriculture directly to services, skipping labour-intensive manufacturing. Lewis model of structural transformation requires manufacturing as intermediate step.\n3. Geographic Concentration: IT boom concentrated in Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, Delhi \u2014 Tier-2/3 cities and rural areas largely excluded.\n4. Gender: Female participation in formal services sectors still low \u2014 domestic work and informal services not counted.\n\nConclusion: Service sector growth is largely sustainable but is NOT a sufficient engine for equitable employment \u2014 India needs labour-intensive manufacturing (PLI schemes) to generate mass employment for the majority."
    },
    {
      id: "q3_d_2017",
      text: "Examine the main economic implications of Goods and Services Tax (GST) in India.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "GST \u2014 Economic Implications:\n\nPositive Implications:\n1. Elimination of Cascading Taxes: GST replaced 17 central and state taxes \u2014 input tax credit chain eliminates tax-on-tax. Estimated savings: 1-2% of GDP in embedded taxes.\n2. Common Market: Inter-state movement of goods free from state border levies \u2014 unified national market. Logistics efficiency gained (30-40% truck time at checkpoints eliminated).\n3. Formalisation: GST return filing requires supplier-customer matching \u2014 incentivises formal invoicing; 1.4 crore new GST registrations.\n4. Revenue Buoyancy: GST collections surpassed \u20b92 lakh crore/month (April 2024) \u2014 self-enforcing mechanism.\n5. Simplified Compliance: Single system replacing multiple state forms \u2014 in theory; in practice, GSTN and GSTR filing still complex.\n\nNegative/Challenges:\n1. Multiple Rates: Four main slabs (5%, 12%, 18%, 28%) plus exemptions and cess \u2014 violates ideal of simplicity; classification disputes.\n2. Petroleum Exclusion: Crude oil, diesel, petrol, ATF outside GST \u2014 major input cost creates inverted duty structure.\n3. MSME Compliance Burden: Monthly GSTR filings, e-invoicing, reconciliation \u2014 onerous for small businesses. Portal technical issues in early years.\n4. Real Estate: Construction sector partially outside GST \u2014 opacity in property transactions.\n5. Revenue Distribution Dispute: Compensation cess mechanism created Centre-state tension post-COVID.\n6. Input Credit Frauds: \u20b950,000+ crore ITC fraud detected \u2014 systemic weakness.\n\nConclusion: GST has substantially fulfilled its economic promise \u2014 creating a unified national market and improving tax buoyancy. Rationalisation of rates and inclusion of petroleum are the next steps."
    },
    {
      id: "q3_e_2017",
      text: "Examine how Demographic Dividend is affected by the lack of investment in the human capital.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "Demographic Dividend and Human Capital:\n\nDemographic Dividend: Period when working-age population is larger than dependent population \u2014 generating savings, investment, and growth (East Asian miracle was partly dividend-driven).\n\nIndia's Window: Working-age population (15-64) peaking around 2045-2050; demographic dividend window 2020-2055.\n\nHow Lack of Human Capital Investment Undermines Dividend:\n\n1. Education Quality Deficit: ASER 2023 \u2014 43% of Class 8 students cannot read Class 2 text. Youth with poor basic skills cannot participate in high-productivity employment \u2014 'dividend' becomes 'burden' if youth are unemployable.\n\n2. Higher Education-Employment Mismatch: NASSCOM data \u2014 80%+ engineering graduates not industry-ready. Graduates without marketable skills cannot generate productivity gains required for dividend.\n\n3. Health Deficits: 35.5% stunted children (NFHS-5) \u2014 permanent cognitive and physical impairment. India's public health spending 2.2% of GDP. Unhealthy workers are less productive, absent more often.\n\n4. Vocational and Skills Gap: Only 5.4% of India's 15-59 population has formal vocational training \u2014 vs. 70% in Germany, 80% in Japan. Without skills, manufacturing and industry cannot absorb the working-age population.\n\n5. Female LFPR: India's female labour force participation (25%) among world's lowest \u2014 half the potential dividend lost. Investment in women's education, childcare, and safety is direct investment in the dividend.\n\n6. NE India and BIMARU States: Low human capital investment in states with highest remaining fertility \u2014 creating a double deficit: large young population + poor human capital.\n\nConclusion: Demographic dividend is not automatic \u2014 it requires sustained, quality investment in education, health, and skills. Without this, India's demographic advantage will become a demographic burden."
    }
