      suggestedAnswer: "Supreme Court Collegium \u2014 Appointment Impasse:\n\nBackground: NJAC (National Judicial Appointments Commission) \u2014 passed as 99th Constitutional Amendment (2014) \u2014 created a 6-member body for judicial appointments including Union Law Minister and 2 eminent persons.\n\nFifth Judges Case (NJAC Case, 2015): Supreme Court (4-1 majority) struck down NJAC \u2014 held it violated 'basic structure' (judicial independence). Restored collegium system.\n\nPost-NJAC Impasse: Government-Collegium tension over appointments:\n1. Government delayed processing collegium recommendations \u2014 some names pending for years.\n2. Collegium reiterated names \u2014 reiterations obligatory on government (established by Second Judges Case).\n3. Government questioned transparency of collegium process \u2014 sought publication of reasons.\n\nCollegium's Response to Impasse (2022-2023):\n1. Transparency Measures: Collegium began publishing its resolutions with brief reasoning on Supreme Court website \u2014 unprecedented move toward openness.\n2. Iterating Recommendations: Collegium reiterated names pending government approval \u2014 forcing government hand.\n3. Supreme Court Interventions: SC (in suo motu) criticised executive delay; bench issued orders mandating time-bound processing.\n\nCurrent Position:\n- Government remains constitutionally bound to act on reiterated recommendations.\n- High Court vacancies (40%+ at times) \u2014 partly attributed to appointment delays.\n- Calls for NJAC 2.0 \u2014 reformed version preserving judicial independence while addressing transparency concerns.\n\nConclusion: The collegium-executive standoff reflects unresolved tension between judicial independence and democratic accountability \u2014 a constitutional dialogue that requires statutory or convention-based resolution without disrupting judicial independence."
