      id: "q4_e_2016",
      text: "Analyse the impact of the Constitution (42nd Amendment) Act on environmental protection and improvement.",
      marks: 5,
      suggestedAnswer: "42nd Constitutional Amendment (1976) and Environment:\n\nContext: 42nd Amendment (during Emergency, 1976) was the most sweeping constitutional amendment \u2014 adding numerous provisions.\n\nEnvironmental Provisions Added:\n1. Article 48A (added to DPSP): 'The State shall endeavour to protect and improve the environment and to safeguard the forests and wild life of the country.'\n2. Article 51A(g) (added to Fundamental Duties): 'It shall be the duty of every citizen of India to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures.'\n\nImpact:\n1. Constitutional Mandate: Environment protection elevated from mere legislative policy to constitutional obligation \u2014 both state and citizens.\n2. Legislative Foundation: Provided constitutional basis for Environment Protection Act (1986), Forest Conservation Act (1980 Amendment), Wildlife Protection Act amendments.\n3. Judicial Interpretation: Supreme Court interpreted Article 21 (right to life) to include right to a clean environment (M.C. Mehta vs. Union of India, 1987) \u2014 with Article 48A as supporting provision.\n4. PIL Jurisprudence: M.C. Mehta, T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad (forest conservation) \u2014 major environmental cases filed under constitutional provisions.\n5. Green Tribunal: National Green Tribunal Act (2010) created, partly building on constitutional environmental mandate.\n\nLimitations:\n- Article 48A is DPSP \u2014 not justiciable on its own; requires judicial creative interpretation.\n- Article 51A duties are non-enforceable without specific legislation.\n\nConclusion: 42nd Amendment gave environmental protection constitutional status \u2014 though its impact depends on judicial interpretation and legislative follow-through rather than direct enforcement."
